# Absolute origin of the deployed site, no trailing slash.
# Everything canonical depends on it: <link rel="canonical">, the hreflang
# alternates, the Open Graph URLs, the sitemap and the JSON-LD.
#
# It must be set in the hosting provider's BUILD environment, not only at
# runtime. These pages are statically generated, so NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL is
# inlined by `next build`; a value added afterwards changes nothing.
#
# The fallback is not localhost. `productionUrl` in lib/site.ts is the
# canonical origin, and a production build with this variable unset uses it,
# so the worst case is the right domain rather than 116 canonicals pointing at
# a developer's laptop. `next dev` still uses http://localhost:3000.
#
# Set it explicitly anyway on production, and set it to the preview host on a
# staging deployment so that staging does not claim production's canonicals.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://privatetransfersacrossitaly.com


# ── Lead database ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing to set. The database is Cloudflare D1 and it arrives as a binding,
# not a credential: `d1_databases` in wrangler.jsonc names it, and the Worker
# runtime hands it to server code through getCloudflareContext().
#
#   binding        DB
#   database_name  italytransfers
#
# There is no URL and no key, which is the one real security advantage over
# the arrangement this replaced: there is no anon key to leak, because there
# is no key. The cost is that D1 has no row-level security, so the guards are
# the server-only binding plus the authorization checks in lib/crm/auth.ts.
# See the header of migrations/0001_create_leads.sql.
#
# Apply the schema with:
#   npx wrangler d1 execute italytransfers --remote --file=migrations/0001_create_leads.sql
#
# While the binding is absent — a non-Cloudflare host, or `next dev` without
# initOpenNextCloudflareForDev — the quotation form still validates and tells
# the visitor plainly that nothing was sent. It never reports a success that
# did not happen.


# ── CRM access ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Authentication is Cloudflare Access. There is no password to store here and
# no login form in the application: Access authenticates at the edge and this
# app verifies the signed JWT it attaches. See docs/cloudflare-access-setup.md
# for the dashboard steps that produce these two values.
#
# Both are server-only. Set them as Worker secrets, never as plain vars:
#   npx wrangler secret put CF_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN
#   npx wrangler secret put CF_ACCESS_AUD
#   npx wrangler secret put CRM_ALLOWED_EMAILS

# e.g. yourteam.cloudflareaccess.com — no scheme, no trailing slash.
CF_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN=
# The Access application's Audience (AUD) tag, 64 hex characters. A token is
# rejected unless this is among its `aud` claims, so a valid token for some
# other Access application of yours cannot open this CRM.
CF_ACCESS_AUD=

# Who may use the CRM. A comma-separated list of email addresses.
#
# Deliberately separate from the Access policy. Access decides who may reach
# the Worker; this decides who may see leads. Widening one by accident does
# not widen the other.
#
# An empty or unset list denies everyone. That is deliberate — a
# misconfigured deployment should lock the CRM, not open it.
CRM_ALLOWED_EMAILS=


# ── Lead email ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Resend, with its own verified sending domain. Not shared with any other
# website. Server-only; set as secrets.
RESEND_API_KEY=
# Must be on a domain verified in THIS Resend account.
LEAD_FROM_EMAIL=Private Transfer Across Italy <quotes@privatetransfersacrossitaly.com>
# Where a new quotation request is announced.
LEAD_ADMIN_EMAIL=
#
# Leave this unset. It is the fallback Reply-To, and the only message that
# uses it is the customer's confirmation — where replies should reach the
# business, which is what the LEAD_ADMIN_EMAIL default already does.
#
# It is NOT how the admin notification gets a customer Reply-To. That is
# already dynamic and cannot be done with a static variable: lib/leads/notify.ts
# passes `replyTo: lead.email` on the admin send, so replying to a new-enquiry
# notification goes straight to the person who filled in the form. Setting
# this variable would only redirect customer replies away from the inbox.
LEAD_REPLY_TO_EMAIL=

# With these unset a lead is still saved; both messages are recorded on the
# lead as "skipped" rather than silently not happening, and the success page
# does not claim a copy was emailed.


# ── Quotation form rate limiting ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional. Counted in the database rather than in process memory, so the
# limit is exact however many isolates are warm — which matters more on
# Workers than on a single Node server. Keyed on the submitted email address,
# not the IP: an IP is shared by a hotel, an office or a whole mobile carrier,
# and limiting on it punishes the wrong people.
# QUOTE_RATE_LIMIT=10
# QUOTE_RATE_WINDOW_MS=600000
